course: dictionary
lesson: 4435
title: Handkerchief: one word, two uses
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A handkerchief is a small square of cloth, or sometimes paper, that you keep in your pocket. You use it to wipe your nose, your eyes or your face. The context tells you which job it does: «he leaves, pressing his handkerchief to his face» shows a handkerchief being used for wiping.
explain: The word has a second meaning. It can also be a square of cloth worn around the neck or head, like a scarf. Check the words around it to decide which meaning fits. Ask yourself: is someone wiping, or is someone wearing it?
explain: Context also helps with the story. In «the Rat ties it up with his handkerchief», the Rat is using the cloth as a bandage for a cut. In «a blue handkerchief», the traveller has tied his things up in a bundle. Swap in your meaning and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: A handkerchief is a small square of cloth or paper.
fact: You carry a handkerchief in your pocket to wipe your nose, eyes or face.
fact: Use a handkerchief or tissue when you sneeze or cough, then wash your hands.
fact: A handkerchief can also be a square of cloth worn around the neck or head, like a scarf.
fact: Short forms of handkerchief are «hanky» and «hankie».
exercise: «The Rat ties it up with his handkerchief» (his shin is hurt). What is the handkerchief used for here? | To wave goodbye | To keep his ears warm | *To tie up a cut | To carry his shoes
exercise: In old stories, people wave handkerchiefs to say what? | It is raining | *Goodbye | Sorry for being late | Hello to the sun
exercise: Which of these is a short form of handkerchief? | handbag | *hanky | handle | hammock
test: «He leaves, pressing his handkerchief to his face.» Why might he do this? | To ask a question | To keep warm in the cold | *To wipe his face, perhaps because he is upset | To make a flag
test: «A faded blue jersey and his things tied up in a blue handkerchief.» What is the handkerchief used for here? | *To carry his things in a bundle | To wipe sweat from his brow | To catch a sneeze | To wear as a scarf
exam: «Busy» in his study, they say, which everyone knows means asleep in his arm-chair with a red handkerchief over his face. What does the handkerchief over his face suggest? | *He is asleep | He is singing loudly | He is cooking dinner | He has gone outside to play
